  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A medieval skull fooled British science for forty-one years. Piltdown, East Sussex. Announced to the Geological Society in December 1912.",
        "visual_query": "sussex english countryside gravel",
        "visual_fallback": "gravel pit"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They named it Eoanthropus dawsoni. Dawson's dawn-man. Half a million years old, they said, and the missing link happened to be English.",
        "visual_query": "old natural history museum",
        "visual_fallback": "museum hall"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It confirmed exactly what everyone already believed: big brain first. So real African fossils got shelved for decades, because a forgery outranked them.",
        "visual_query": "african savanna excavation dig",
        "visual_fallback": "fossil dig"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then in 1953, fluorine testing broke it apart. Human skull. Orangutan jaw. Chimpanzee teeth, filed flat and stained with iron to match.",
        "visual_query": "vintage laboratory glassware testing",
        "visual_fallback": "laboratory bench"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A 2016 study traced both Piltdown sites to one orangutan and one man: Charles Dawson, the amateur who found it. Forty years of textbooks, built on a fake.",
        "visual_query": "museum fossil skull display",
        "visual_fallback": "skull display"
      }
    ],
    "segment_count": 5,
    "word_count": 115,
    "duration_seconds": 35.0,
