        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Arthur Smith Woodward named the specimen Eoanthropus dawsoni and estimated it at about 500,000 years old, presenting it as a missing link.",
        "segment_index": 1,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "79% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 1 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Piltdown Man fitted the then-popular view that a large brain evolved first, and genuine finds such as Raymond Dart's Taung Child were initially overlooked because of it.",
        "segment_index": 2,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "80% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Fluorine absorption dating exposed the forgery: a medieval human skull, an orangutan lower jaw and chimpanzee teeth, file-marked and stained with iron solution and chromic acid.",
        "segment_index": 3,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "79% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "A 2016 study combining morphology and DNA concluded one person made the specimens, most likely Charles Dawson, with teeth from both Piltdown sites coming from the same orangutan.",
